1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,480
The reflection design pattern is something I've used in many applications, and it's surprisingly easy to implement.

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:07,720
Let's take a look.

3
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:14,780
Just as humans will sometimes reflect their own output and find a way to improve it, so can OMS.

4
00:00:14,980 --> 00:00:21,360
For example, I might write an email like this, and if I'm typing quickly, I might end up with a first draft that's not great.

5
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:28,520
And if I read over it, I might say, huh, next month isn't that clear for what dates Tommy might be free for dinner,

6
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:37,200
and this is your typo that I had, and also forgot to sign my name, and this would let me revise the draft to be more specific in saying,

7
00:00:37,280 --> 00:00:40,020
Hey Tommy, are you free for dinner on the 5th to the 7th?

8
00:00:40,300 --> 00:00:43,960
A similar process lets OMS also improve their output.

9
00:00:44,340 --> 00:00:50,240
You can prompt them to write the first draft in an email, and given email version 1, email v1,

10
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:55,840
you can post it to maybe the same model, the same large language model, but with a different prompt,

11
00:00:55,840 --> 00:01:02,380
and tell it to reflect and write an improved second draft to then get you the final output email v2.

12
00:01:02,540 --> 00:01:10,280
Here, I have just hard coded this workflow of prompting the alum once, and then prompting the alum again to reflect and improve,

13
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:12,460
and that gives email v2.

14
00:01:12,620 --> 00:01:18,120
It turns out that a similar process can be used to improve other types of output.

15
00:01:18,420 --> 00:01:24,660
For example, if you are having an alum write code, you might prompt an alum to write code to do a certain task,

16
00:01:24,660 --> 00:01:32,060
and it may give you v1 of the code, and then pass it to the same alum or maybe a different alum,

17
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:36,460
to ask it to check the bugs and write an improved second draft of the code.

18
00:01:36,660 --> 00:01:42,940
Different LMs have different strengths, and so sometimes I would choose different models for writing the first draft,

19
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:46,360
and for reflecting and trying to improve it.

20
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:50,280
For example, it turns out reasoning models, sometimes also called thinking models,

21
00:01:50,280 --> 00:01:57,420
are pretty good at finding bugs, and so I'll sometimes write the first draft of the code by direct generation,

22
00:01:57,700 --> 00:02:00,340
but then use the reasoning model to check for bugs.

23
00:02:00,580 --> 00:02:07,460
Now, rather than just having an alum reflect on the code, it turns out that if you can get external feedback,

24
00:02:08,060 --> 00:02:13,900
meaning new information from outside the alum, reflection becomes much more powerful.

25
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:20,140
In the case of code, one thing you can do is just execute the code to see what the code does,

26
00:02:20,260 --> 00:02:23,920
and by examining the output, including any error messages of the code,

27
00:02:24,160 --> 00:02:29,820
this is incredibly useful information for the LM to reflect and to find a way to improve his code.

28
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:36,180
So in this example, the LM generated the first draft of the code, but when I run it, it generates a syntax error.

29
00:02:36,180 --> 00:02:44,020
When you pass this code output in error logs back into the LM and ask it to reflect on the feedback and write a new draft,

30
00:02:44,220 --> 00:02:50,180
this gives it a lot of very useful information to come up with a much better version 2 of the code.

31
00:02:50,460 --> 00:02:53,080
So the reflection design pattern isn't magic.

32
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:57,160
It does not make an LM always get everything right 100% of the time,

33
00:02:57,320 --> 00:03:01,020
but it can often give it maybe a modest bump in performance.

34
00:03:01,020 --> 00:03:07,180
But one design consideration to keep in mind is, reflection is much more powerful

35
00:03:07,180 --> 00:03:12,780
when there is new additional external information that you can ingest into the reflection process.

36
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:18,100
So in this example, if you can run the code and have that code output or error messages

37
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:23,280
as an additional input to the reflection step, that really lets the LM reflect much more deeply

38
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:29,180
and figure out what may be going wrong, if anything, and results in a much better second version of the code

39
00:03:29,180 --> 00:03:32,280
than if there wasn't just external information that you can ingest.

40
00:03:32,460 --> 00:03:37,740
So one thing to keep in mind, whenever reflection has an opportunity to get additional information,

41
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,100
that makes it much more powerful.

42
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:46,620
Now with that, let's go on to the next video where I want to share with you a more systematic comparison

43
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:53,960
of using reflection versus direct generation or something we sometimes call zero shot prompting.

44
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:55,800
Let's go on to the next video.

45
00:03:55,800 --> 00:03:55,860
Let's go.

46
00:03:56,360 --> 00:03:56,600
Let's go.

47
00:03:56,600 --> 00:03:56,700
Let's go.

48
00:03:56,700 --> 00:03:57,080
Let's go.

